          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a condition of the whole joint, not just the cartilage. Over time, the cartilage that cushions the joint is progressively lost, and the bone underneath changes in response. It most often affects the knees, hips, hands and spine. The reference summary describes the process as active, involving inflammation and attempted repair, rather than simple wear and tear. (Source: *Osteoarthritis*, general clinical reference.)\n\nOn whether it is the same as arthritis: the retrieved material does not directly say that osteoarthritis is one type of arthritis, so I can't confirm that from these sources. What the sources do show is that osteoarthritis is classified separately from the inflammatory joint conditions. In the ICD-10-CM coding system, rheumatoid arthritis and gout sit in an inflammatory group (M05 to M14), while osteoarthritis has its own block (M15 to M19). (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 13: musculoskeletal system*.) A clinician can explain how the terms relate in more detail.\n\nFor management, the guidance describes exercise as the first-line treatment, combining muscle strengthening, range-of-movement work and aerobic activity. Medicines are described as add-ons to exercise, not replacements. (Source: *Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management*.) Please discuss any treatment choices with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
